<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the League of Cognac</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1526">1526</a>–<a href="/years/1530">1530</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the League of Cognac was fought from 1526 to 1530 between Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> and an alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Pope Clement VII</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> formed to limit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Imperial troops sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1527, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-cambrai">Peace of Cambrai</a> (1529) and the fall of Florence (1530) left Charles dominant in Italy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-league-of-cognac.jpg" alt="War of the League of Cognac" width="320" height="170" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giorgio Vasari · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siege_of_Florence.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Alarmed by the French defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Italian War</a> of 1521, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Pope Clement VII</a> and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> set about building an alliance to drive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Francis I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had been captured at Pavia and had signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madrid-1526">Treaty of Madrid</a>, leaving his two eldest sons in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as surety. Once released he repudiated the treaty as signed under duress and promised to help the pope.</p><p>The League of Cognac was formed on 22 May 1526 in the French town. Its members were Francis, who wanted revenge for his defeat, the pope, whose authority had been challenged over benefices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, the republics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, which feared a larger imperial invasion, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-ii-sforza">Francesco II Sforza</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, whose duchy was under imperial occupation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII of England</a> refused to join.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>A month after its formation the League seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a>, but imperial troops marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and forced Sforza to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. In September 1526 the Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and Cardinal Pompeo Colonna defeated the papal forces and briefly held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Charles V then gathered 14,000 German landsknechte under Georg Frundsberg and 6,000 Spanish soldiers under the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, who advanced on Rome. When Bourbon fell in the assault, the underpaid imperial troops mutinied and sacked the city. The pope took refuge in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-santangelo">Castel Sant'Angelo</a>, his flight made possible by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swiss-guards">Swiss Guards</a>' last stand.</p><p>In August 1527 a French army under the Viscount of Lautrec entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-doria">Andrea Doria</a>, and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on 29 April 1528. Doria then defected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, plague broke out in the French camps, killing Lautrec, and the remnant of the army capitulated at Aversa in August 1528. A second French army under the Count of Saint-Pol was defeated at Landriano on 21 June 1529.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Negotiations at Cambrai were conducted mainly by Francis's mother, Louise of Savoy, and Charles's aunt, Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, so the treaty was called the Peace of the Ladies. Signed on 3 August 1529, it required Francis to renounce his claims to Artois, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a> and to pay a ransom of two million gold écus for his sons.</p><p>At Bologna Clement VII gave absolution to those who had sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and promised to crown Charles King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a> was allowed to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on payment of 900,000 scudi. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> alone kept resisting. In 1530 imperial forces won the battle of Gavinana, in which the Prince of Orange was killed, and the republic capitulated ten days later. The pope installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-de-medici">Alessandro de' Medici</a> as Duke of Florence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the League of Cognac” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Antonio de Leyva's Imperial–Spanish army destroyed the French army of the Count of Saint-Pol at Landriano in the <b>War of the League of Cognac</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-21">21 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
